# How to use the backlog (humans and agents)

What is left to do on {{ title }}, what is done, and what was turned down. **This folder is the
only up-to-date source.**

| File | Content |
|---|---|
| [README.md](README.md) | The index: north star, next free ID, pointers (no state a command can compute) |
| [tickets/](tickets/) | **Everything open**: one file per ticket, `{{ name }}-T-NN-<slug>.md` |
| [releases/](releases/) | One file per planned release, `<release-name>.md`: version, why, handoff; its tickets say they belong to it |
| [done.md](done.md) | The closed tickets, one line each, most recent first |
| [rejected.md](rejected.md) | What was turned down, dropped, or ruled out for good |
| [urgent.md](urgent.md) | What only a human on the team can do (access, payment, decision), one checkbox per action |

An audit writes its dated report next to these files (`audit-<topic>-YYYY-MM-DD.md`) while it
runs, copies its open findings into tickets (with a pointer to their lines), then moves to
`docs/_archive/YYYY-MM-DD-topic/`. Archives **can be read, but everything in them is old and must
be checked** against the current code; they are never edited.

## A ticket

One file, `tickets/{{ name }}-T-NN-<slug>.md` (`<slug>`: a few lowercase words of the title, set
when the ticket is written and kept if the title changes): a heading, the fixed lines, then the
details. The ticket is the brief of a worker (often a Sonnet subagent) who starts with no
context: it names the files, so the worker never explores to find them.

    # {{ name }}#T-NN · Short title that says what to achieve
    - **Type**: task
    - **Priority**: 🟡 normal
    - **Status**: todo
    - **Opened**: YYYY-MM-DD
    - **Effort**: S · **Owner**: … · **Due**: —
    - **Session**: sonnet medium
    - **Touches**: backend events; migration no; API contract yes; front crew/
    - **Release**: <release-name>
    - **Origin**: where it comes from · audit `docs/_archive/<folder>/README.md` l. 10-20

    Why, what to do (as a `- [ ]` checklist when there are several steps).

    ## Files
    - `path/to/module.py` — edit
    - `path/to/rules.py` — source of truth (read it, never duplicate it)
    - `path/to/generated.ts` — generated by `<command>` (never edited by hand)
    - `tests/test_module.py` — test (to write or extend; expected values for money, migrations, personal data)

    ## Done when
    What is checked before the merge.

- **ID** `{{ name }}#T-NN`: stable, never reused, always written whole, in this repo too, and
  quoted as is in commits and pull requests (a branch carries the number without the prefix, then what it holds: `fix/t07-season-guide`). The
  prefix is this repo's name; a ticket of another repo carries that repo's name
  (`other-repo#T-07`). The next free one is written in [README.md](README.md)
  (`Next free: {{ name }}#T-NN`), moved on by each new ticket. If the repo is renamed,
  `ion repo set name=…` moves its own prefix here, file names included, same numbers; citations
  in other repos keep the old name, which the forge redirects, and git history is never
  rewritten.
- **Display name** `{{ name }}#T-NN_vX.Y.Z_<slug>` (no `_vX.Y.Z` without a planned release): the
  name of a session, computed by `ion repo session` from `releases/`. Never written in a file name,
  a commit or a link: a ticket changes release, its ID does not.
- **Type**, one of: `bug` (something is broken); `improvement` (it works, it could work
  better); `idea` (nothing exists yet, to be explored); `task` (work that is neither: a release,
  a decision, a check).
- **Priority**, one of: `🔴 critical` (now), `🟠 important` (before the north star),
  `🟡 normal`, `⚪ background` (long-running work, debt, ideas still to be explored).
- **Status**, one of: `todo`; `in progress — branch <name>, since YYYY-MM-DD`;
  `blocked — waiting for <who or what>`; `to verify — <where>`.
- **Effort**: XS (under an hour), S (half a day), M (one or two days), L (more).
- **Session**: `<model> <effort>`: the model of the Claude Code session that works the ticket
  (`sonnet` by default, `opus` when the work needs it) and its effort (`low`, `medium`, `high`,
  `xhigh`, `max`); `, <branch>` may end it. Without the line, the model is `sonnet`, XS and S
  give `low`, M and L `medium`, the branch `<type>/tNN-<slug>`. The former
  `· **Session**: medium, fix/two-coaches` at the end of the Effort line is still read, and
  `ion repo update` moves it to its own line. `ion repo session {{ name }}#T-NN` prints the line
  that opens it; `ion repo session --subagent {{ name }}#T-NN` prints the brief an orchestrator
  gives the `ticket` subagent.
- **Touches**: what the ticket changes (backend apps, migration yes or no, API contract yes or
  no, front folders): an orchestrator reads it to know which tickets may run at the same time.
- **Release**: the name of the release file the ticket is planned in (`releases/<name>.md`),
  the only place that says it. Absent: the ticket is unplanned.
- **Files**: one line per path with its role: `edit`; `source of truth` (where the value or rule
  lives: read it, never duplicate it); `generated by <command>` (never edited by hand); `test`
  (to write or extend, with the expected values for money, migrations and personal data). The
  paths are checked when the ticket is written; the worker still verifies them, but no longer
  explores to find them.
- **Done when**: what the orchestrator checks before merging.
- **Owner**: `—` (free, anyone may take it), a member's GitHub login (`@login`) or first name
  (the ticket is reserved for them), or `contributor — #NN` (kept for an outside contributor);
  never `Claude`: an agent is not an owner. An agent only works on a free ticket or one owned
  by the account it works for (`gh api user --jq .login`): it skips the others and, asked for
  one, says who owns it instead of doing it. **Each member writes only their own login**:
  taking a free ticket, or turning their own first name into their login; never someone else's
  (only the owner hands a ticket over).
- **Closing a ticket**: delete its file (its release file lists nothing to edit), and add a line
  at the top of [done.md](done.md):
  `- **YYYY-MM-DD** · {{ name }}#T-NN · Title — what was done (branch) · opened YYYY-MM-DD`.
- **Details in archived audits**: when a ticket quotes lines of `docs/_archive/`, the original
  finding is there in full; it is old, check it against the current code.
- **Map**: `ion repo backlog` prints production's version (read from git), then each release in
  version order with its tickets (ID, status, size, session, touches, title), then the unplanned
  tickets; `--release <name>` prints one release. Read only the files of the tickets involved.

## A release

One file, `releases/<release-name>.md`, written by `/repo:backlog plan`. Its name is a stable
slug of its title (a few lowercase words), kept when the version changes; the version is one
line, so renumbering edits that line and moves no file. It lists no ticket: each ticket names
its release in its `Release` line, and `ion repo backlog` draws the plan. Numbers are planned,
not promised, until the candidate is cut:

    # What the user notices once it ships
    - **Version**: vX.Y.Z

    Why these tickets go together, in one sentence.

    ## Handoff
    Done, in progress, next: overwritten by the orchestrator at each relay.

The index does not list releases either: `ion repo backlog` does. A shipped release leaves the
folder.
